    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 042018\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n118 PM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Major waterways such as the Salt and Gila Rivers will continue \n  to experience high volumes flows through at least the end of the\n  week due to dam releases.\n\n- With the exception of a slight chances of mountain showers to \n  finish out the workweek, dry weather will be common through at \n  least the next seven days. \n\n- Temperatures a few degrees below normal will warm very close to\n  seasonal readings by this weekend.\n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Friday/...\nAfternoon objective analysis reveals a rather dynamic pattern over \nthe CONUS with closed lows spinning near the Utah-Colorado border \nand upper Midwest. These disturbances are now in close enough \nproximity to where they have severed an omega block type setup that \nbrought short-lived ridging to the Desert Southwest yesterday. \nHowever, the western most system will only flatten out heights \naloft across Arizona and southern California, which will promote\ntranquil conditions through Thursday. The pattern will take on a \nbit more cyclonic curvature by Friday as a decaying shortwave \ndives south from the Pacific Northwest. Guidance points towards a \nslight dip in the height fields as a result of the associated \nascent aloft, but, as mentioned in the previous discussion, \nconfidence remains low on exactly where this potential trough will\nsetup. Regardless of positioning, intensity will likely be \nlacking, which would result in only some minimal to minor impacts \nareawide. The most expected from this system as of now is breezy \nconditions for portions of the region Friday into Saturday and \nperhaps some terrain driven shower activity Friday afternoon.\n\nDay-to-day temperatures the next few afternoons will run relatively\nsteady thanks to the above-mentioned zonal flow. Lower desert \nhighs will range generally in the mid to upper 70s with perhaps a \nfew spots reaching into the lower 80s, around 3-5 degrees below \nnormal for the start of November. Even with the above-mentioned \ndisturbance, highs on Friday will actually trend upwards due to\nefficient lower troposhperic heating, hovering within a degree or\ntwo of seasonal levels by that point.\n\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Saturday through Tuesday/... \nDuring the weekend, some semblance of a Rex block (or omega block) \nwill develop in the southern stream with weak troughing over \nnorthern Mexico and broad ridging across the Great Basin/central \nRockies. While the precise orientation and positioning of salient \nfeatures remain uncertain based on notable ensemble differences, the \noverall pattern will promote surface pressure rises over the \nintermountain West and dry N/NE boundary layer flow. Based on the \npattern, periods of breezy northeast winds are likely at times over \nthe weekend and early next week, though nothing particularly strong \nor unusual for this time of year. At some point early next week, the \nbulk of ensemble members eject the northern Mexico wave through the \nforecast area bringing enhanced mid/upper level moisture. It remains \nto be seen whether this process can overcome the dry boundary layer \nairmass, with the highest odds suggesting dry weather persisting. \nWith H5 heights remaining nearly steady state in a 580-584dm range, \ntemperatures should follow a near persistence trends with readings \nnot terribly far from climatology. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1740Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT; and \nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo aviation weather concerns are expected through the forecast \nperiod under mostly clear skies. Winds will remain light \n(generally 7 kts or less) and follow typical diurnal patterns at \nthe Phoenix Metro terminals. Wind directions at KBLH and KIPL \nwill generally remain N-NW through tonight. Extended periods of \ncalm and vrb conditions can also be expected at all terminals, \nespecially during any diurnal wind shifts.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nWhile a stray shower may affect mountains of eastern districts \nFriday, the area will remain dry with little to no chance of wetting \nrainfall through early next week. Minimum afternoon humidity levels \nwill largely fall into a 20-30% range in western districts and 30-\n50% in eastern districts. This will follow fair to good overnight \nrecovery in western districts and good to excellent levels in eastern \ndistricts. Winds speeds will remain seasonably light for the majority\nof the period, albeit obtaining 20-30 mph northerly gusts through\nthe lower Colorado River valley late in the week. Increased easterly\nwinds will also be likely in eastern districts early next week. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...RW\nLONG TERM...18 \nAVIATION...Salerno \nFIRE WEATHER...18\n",
